  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A defaulted land deal gave William Levitt his start in housebuilding. His father, Abraham, formed Levitt & Sons to develop the property; William managed the business while his brother, Alfred, designed the homes. The partnership combined commercial organisation with architectural work. William bought out Alfred and later took the company public, retaining a controlling stake.\n\nThe 1968 sale to ITT exchanged the business for shares, leaving Levitt exposed when their value fell. He borrowed against the stock to pursue overseas projects, while later attempts to rebuild his American development business also failed. By the late 1980s, he had been forced to liquidate his assets. He was still contemplating a comeback shortly before his death in 1994.",
    "html": "<p>A defaulted land deal gave William Levitt his start in housebuilding. His father, Abraham, formed Levitt &amp; Sons to develop the property; William managed the business while his brother, Alfred, designed the homes. The partnership combined commercial organisation with architectural work. William bought out Alfred and later took the company public, retaining a controlling stake.</p>\r\n<p>The 1968 sale to ITT exchanged the business for shares, leaving Levitt exposed when their value fell. He borrowed against the stock to pursue overseas projects, while later attempts to rebuild his American development business also failed. By the late 1980s, he had been forced to liquidate his assets. He was still contemplating a comeback shortly before his death in 1994.</p>\r\n",
